          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A newborn is assessed at the end of the Golden Minute. The baby is not breathing</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A newborn is assessed at the end of the Golden Minute. The baby is not breathing and has a heart rate of 90 beats per minute. What is the correct immediate action?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Continue observation for another 60 seconds</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Delaying intervention is dangerous. The neonatal brain tolerates hypoxia very poorly — neuronal death begins within 4–6 minutes of complete oxygen deprivation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Initiate chest compressions using the two-thumb technique</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chest compressions are a later-line intervention, indicated only when heart rate remains below 60 bpm after 30 seconds of confirmed effective positive-pressure ventilation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Begin positive-pressure ventilation (PPV) immediately</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The NRP mandates that if the baby is not breathing or has a heart rate below 100 bpm at the end of the Golden Minute, PPV must begin immediately. Ventilation is always the first priority.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Administer intravenous adrenaline</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adrenaline is reserved for cases where heart rate remains below 60 bpm despite adequate ventilation and chest compressions. It is never the first-line intervention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The NRP Golden Minute protocol requires immediate PPV if the baby is apnoeic or has HR < 100 bpm. Ventilation is the first-line intervention — compressions and adrenaline come later, only if PPV fails."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The Golden Minute principle is clear: apnoea or HR < 100 bpm at 60 seconds demands immediate PPV. Most neonates respond to effective ventilation alone."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
